ty  but a man's life was at stake here so  they finally decided they had to do  something so they met with reverend  shuttlesworth warned him against  going to a  airport restaurant where uh  shuttlesworth was going to try to  desegregate it  because that's where the assassination  was going to take place solosworth was  very grateful he didn't go there that  night and and he's still alive today  so occasionally the fbi would act on the  information and would prevent violence  but in rose case uh it seems to have  been uh rare that the bureau actually  prevented  violence from from occurring  i wish i could be more confident about  the thousands of other people who became  informants inside the clan  i can't because historians journalists  interested in this subject can't get  access to  informant files at the fbi  they are exempt from the freedom of  information act  uh the fbi guards those those records uh  you know very very carefully for obvious  reasons for what it reveals about  their complicity in in in so many events  i was fortunate because  in the in the case of of the lyouzo  murder  and the later wrongful death lawsuit the  liuso family attorney received  rose informant files from the fbi  through the discovery process and the  the the lawyer dean rob was kind enough  to give me access to those records  so at least i can i can tell you one  case where actually prevent violence but  i can't speak about the thousands of  others  sir  is rose still alive and if so did you  also have contact with him while you  were preparing your phone ro died in in  1998  he spent his last years in savannah  georgia where he was was born  he working for a private detective firm  it is said that he died of of natural  causes  although i  managed to locate one of rose  children from a very early marriage  and she told me that  she thought her father had been murdered  and the story she told me was was so  incredible  he'd been working as a private detective  he'd come across