id interview about her  mother  yes sir  on balance having listened to you now on  balance i'm not sure did this man do  more good than harm  i'm not sure myself um although i really  should take a position i think on this  i would have to conclude that he  probably did more harm  than good  the reverend shuttlesworth might  disagree with me but  certainly as i say the the the  information that he passed along  uh was so rarely acted upon by the fbi  that um it just does not seem to have  been  worth  the trouble that was caused and in fact  roe himself  in later years said that he thought it  was probably unnecessary to infiltrate  the klan to put informants inside the  clan he said  there are probably about a dozen people  inside the clan who were the most  dangerous and if the fbi had kept him  under surveillance  so much could have been prevented but it  wasn't really necessary uh to to  infiltrate the klan  what was so interesting is you know we  have this perception of what the clan is  like and  this clan group that i studied which was  considered among the most dangerous was  a kind of combination of the keystone  cops and the manson family uh i mean  they were capable of of the silliest  sorts of things  yet at the same time a few of them  were vicious  killers  yes  it doesn't sound like the fbi has much  of a good history until today  well again uh i think a historian has  got to look at the entire record here  and in fact in in my research  what i found was that the  the the field agents especially the the  the major case inspectors who were  called in after uh mrs liuzo was killed  uh the ones who um  search for the killers of cheney  schwerner and goodman  these were first-rate people uh they  weren't uh racist like j edgar hoover  was  they were not out to destroy the civil  rights movement like jager hoover was  they were serious investigators  and they worked very hard  and they're not well known to the public  so we have to be fair i think the higher  up one went in in the bureau uh you  found the more serious problems  especially uh in in hoover himself  yes  you called um  hoover a racist and so can did your  research reveal  can you talk a little bit about what  your research may have revealed in terms  of relationship between lbj and also  hoover were there any nuggets that you  found and did lbj were they using each  other for different purposes uh  it's a wonderful question um  we have now a tremendous  and rich resource in in the lbj tapes  which are available to to all citizens  here's lbj with the bark off uh he had  tape  machinery on all his telephones and and  just all over the place uh  and  you you really hear him uh expressing  himself very frankly what's fascinating  is if you look at the conversations  between hoover and lbj  you discover that that not only was lbj  taping hoover hoover was taping lbj  they had an interesting relationship  they've known each other for a long time  and after all johnson first went to  washington in 1931  when hoover himself was was a young  director of the fbi  um  for a time they actually i think lived  across the street from one another  lbj also recognized that hoover was a  man that you you couldn't cross  uh i suppose on the one hand he would  have loved to have gotten rid of him  finally fired him i mean hoover was you  know the longest serving  director of the fbi in history and  probably one of the the longest serving  federal bureaucrats  itself  but he couldn't johnson couldn't kennedy  had thought of doing that but he knew  that hoover had so much stuff in his  files  about president kennedy about lyndon  johnson too  lyndon johnson was as much uh  a womanizer as jfk was and and those  records were in fbi files i didn't see  any of that uh  some of huber's most sensitive files  were burned by his private secretary  after his death  uh i think that they did use one another  certainly uh  hoover wanted johnson to protect him  from his critics to allow him as johnson  did to let his empire  grow  as it did  and at the same time johnson used hoover  knowing that he was  at least in the 60s one of the most  respected men in the country it's only  later of course that we we now have  revelations about what hoover was all  about but i think i know i'm speaking  for myself uh every boy growing up at  one time or another at least in the time  i was growing up wanted to become an fbi  agent  and not long before tommy rowe became an  informant one of the most popular films  in the country was the fbi story with  jimmy stewart  so um  so we now know more about hoover and the  johnson hoover relationship is is a  fascinating one great question  yes ma'am  did you ever get the feeling that  roe really needed to be  rescued from himself  it seems that the fbi we're really  creating more of a monster  because  it sounds as if a person with such a  personality it sounds like he might have  really been very very power hungry  and  the fbi was really feeding his ego  well the fbi uh did feed his ego and  they wanted him  to to rise up within the klan in order  to get more and more important  information  yet they recognized that he was someone  who could be very difficult to control  and again what was fascinating in  examining these records as roe became  such an important informant  and his contact agents would would  retire from the bureau  and their replacements would be uh  selected  the agents actually auditioned for roe  uh you know they were they recognized  how important role was and their own  careers as fbi agents were dependent on  their ability to cultivate and create  informants so you have a case of of  someone retiring his rose contact agent  selecting his successor and the  successor saying well i sure hope he's  going to like me you know i i think  he'll like me we've met we've had coffee  and i think we'll get along fine so it's  it's a fascinating relationship uh it  was one of the most interesting things i  think uh in the book you know the  relationship between an informant and  his handler this this kind of very  strange sort of tension um  and in some cases a very close  relationship  when rose's first contact agent retired  uh in uh in 1961 he was broken up i mean  he was he was just uh  falling apart and he wanted to quit  but his success the the agent's  successor said no tommy you've got to  stay you're doing a great job for the  country and of course i'm not sure was  entirely serious about leaving uh the  informant world because it was so much  fun  but he was very disturbed that his you  know his first agent was going to go off  and leave him  yes sir will you comment on  how the fbi's undercover activities  compared to other spy organizations that  are undercover well  a wonderful question which i i wish i  could answer  i indicated earlier that  informant files are  off limits to historians that they're  exempt from the freedom of information  act so historians can't get their hands  on informant files i was lucky because  in the leisure case it was a wrongful  death lawsuit and the fbi had to give  their files to  the user family attorney who gave me  access to them  so it's hard for me to answer that um  i i think it's it's probably similar  though to  spies who are run for example by the by  the c i a  or even in other  international intelligence services  they have to pick these people who  they can control  yet they have to be  have a certain strength and power to to  live this kind of life and it takes a  person i think who's a little on the odd  side  as roe was to uh to live a kind of  double life some people i guess those  who are drawn to the secret world of  international intelligence or domestic  intelligence  love it they love that excitement of  pretending to be something that they're  not  i wish i had the records to confidently  answer that question it's a great one  yes ma'am  you said that there were  or that rose said that if they uh kind  of kept track of 12 people within the  clan  uh that would have been okay and they  wouldn't have needed informants but i'm  trying to understand how they would have  known those 12 people if they didn't  have informants  and where i'm going with this is i'm  going to the issue of terrorism now  where if they're saying oh we really  need informants and people are saying  well we don't need informants and some  people are saying we don't need  informants and they're saying well if we  could just track down x number of people  you know that would give us insight so  could you kind of answer the question  about 12 in the clan um take it to the  terrorists yes yes um  in one of the reviews of my book uh the  reviewer  said that i was opposed to all  informants and and that's not true i  think it was a misreading of what i had  written  um it seemed to me that it was more  dangerous to recruit informants from the  outside and then to put them  inside the clan or any other terrorist  group  because  the terrorists the klansmen become  immediately suspicious about this person  and roe in fact was challenged several  times accused of being an fbi informant  and he was able to either fight or talk  his way  out of it  in the case of the clan and i think it's  much  easier  identifying who were the most dangerous  people than it is in in the world now of  of international terrorism  in the case of the clan it was obvious i  mean there was one  one man named robert chambliss who was  eventually convicted of of being the  main bomber  in the 16th street baptist church case  everybody knew  dynamite bob that was his nickname  uh i mean he'd been around birmingham  for 20 years as this you know in and out  of jail in in time protected  actually by bill connery he worked for  the birmingham police force for a time  uh so it was obvious  you didn't need uh to put somebody  inside the clan to answer your question  more specifically in in the case for  example of the mississippi burning case  in 1964  the fbi was able to solve that case  by offering money to klansmen  who could identify the killers lead the  fbi to the location of the bodies  it seemed to me that's the effective way  to go about this to use money or  whatever you have to to turn people who  are already members  of terrorist groups  easy to do i think with the clan  but very very difficult with terrorist  groups of the kind that we're now  dealing with these are obviously  committed  ideologues who  um are quite willing to give their life  for their their cause  yes  once  once they had evidence  from this guy that bull connor  was operating with the clan  what reason was them  what reason was there for them not to  use that evidence against him  um  they  had no position  regarding conor um  some agents like connor  some agents had um  grown up in the south  some had gone to the south married  southern women and tended to be kind of  sympathetic to segregation  and didn't have any problem at all with  with conor i couldn't find any evidence  that that uh that hoover you know  disliked conor um i mean they probably  had a lot in common you know they were  both a tough law and order men  did i answer your question i'm not not  sure i did i guess in practical terms  you probably did i mean law and order  men don't real law and order men don't  allow tell people you can commit a crime  for 15 minutes  i know i don't know  but okay i think we have time for one  more question  yes ma'am  other than being a fascinating  historical  story do you see  uh any of this being relevant to the  problems of today and i don't just mean  the terrorists but also the far right  militias  well i think it it does um  primarily again in terms of how do you  get information about these groups how  do you develop informants do you put  them inside the klan or other terrorist  groups or militia groups do you try to  turn people who are already inside so i  think that at least i hope the book does  speak  to some of these problems  but it's also i think a terrific story  i'm primarily a storyteller and  always wanted to  to write in a way that's accessible to  readers there's no point it seems to me  for a historian to write for the six or  seven other scholars who are in the  field and you have to have an audience  out there like all of you good people so  that's what i've tried to do thank you  thank you  gary may is author of un-american  activities the trials of william  remington  and china's scapegoat the diplomatic  ordeal of john carter vincent  his latest the informant is published by  yale university press on the web at  yalebooks.com
 

